      "output": "Both deliverables are in place.\n\n## Summary\n\nI profiled `data/seattle-weather.csv` with the `eda` skill's `scripts/profile.py` (keyed on `date`), producing `artifacts/data-profile.json` (untouched, schema-conformant) and extending `artifacts/data-profile.md` with prose the script doesn't cover. I also wrote `artifacts/scratch/column-profile.csv` and displayed it via `show_table`.\n\n**Key facts:**\n- 1,461 rows \u00d7 6 columns, **zero nulls anywhere**, 0% exact duplicates, `date` unique at daily grain.\n- Full daily coverage 2012-01-01 \u2192 2015-12-31 with **no missing days** (1,461 expected = 1,461 observed, including the 2012 leap day).\n- `precipitation` is right-skewed (206 rows, 14.1%, beyond 1.5\u00d7IQR) \u2014 mean (3.03) is not representative alone.\n- `wind` has 34 outlier rows (2.3%); `temp_max`/`temp_min` have none, and `temp_max \u2265 temp_min` holds for every row.\n- `weather` is a clean 5-level categorical (rain, sun, fog, drizzle, snow), no unexpected labels.\n\n**Flags for the next step (not gate calls, just noted):**\n- Cross-column oddity: `precipitation` is exactly 0.0 for every `drizzle`, `fog`, and `sun` day (794 rows) \u2014 those labels carry no measured rain in this dataset. Also, 44 of 641 `rain` days (6.9%) have `precipitation == 0.0`. Worth knowing if downstream analysis treats `weather` and `precipitation` as interchangeable signals.\n- Units for `wind`, `temp_max/min`, `precipitation` are inferred (m/s, \u00b0C, mm) from typical ranges, not documented in source \u2014 flagged under \"what this data cannot answer.\"\n- No location/station field to confirm this is a single Seattle site.\n\nNo duplicate or null issues, so I expect the data-gate step to pass on those checks; the precipitation outlier share is a distributional characteristic, not a defect, but I've surfaced it for the gate owner to weigh if relevant.",
