      "output": "Done. Summary of what I did and handoff notes for the report step:\n\n**What I did**\n- Read the gate report (`GATE: PASS`, no nulls/dupes/gaps, 1,461 daily rows, 2012\u20132015) and the profile before touching the data, so I knew precipitation is right-skewed and `precipitation`/`weather` disagree on some rows.\n- Computed category-level stats (precipitation median/IQR, mean temp_max/temp_min) across `drizzle`, `fog`, `rain`, `snow`, `sun`.\n- For the 2012\u21922015 change, fit OLS trends (with 95% CIs, n=4 annual points, df=2) on annual mean temp_max, temp_min, mean precipitation, and category-share series \u2014 reporting \"no clear trend\" wherever the CI crossed zero (precipitation, rain share, snow share) rather than asserting a direction, and only calling out temperature and fog share as real trends since those CIs stayed on one side of zero.\n- Produced 5 Vega-Lite charts from small aggregates under `artifacts/scratch/`: precipitation-by-category (bar+IQR rule), temperature-by-category (grouped bars), annual temperature trend (pan/zoom line), annual precipitation trend (pan/zoom line), and category-share-by-year (interactive line with hover).\n- Wrote `artifacts/findings.md` with exactly the three required sections (3 headline findings, evidence tied to chart titles with denominators, caveats sourced only from the gate report).\n\n**Concerns for the report step**\n- The rain/snow \"decline\" is visually obvious but statistically unconfirmed at 4 years of data (p=0.085 and 0.152) \u2014 the report should keep that hedge rather than upgrading it to a clean trend.\n- Snow's yearly counts (21, 3, 2, 0) are small; I kept it out of any headline claim of magnitude and only used it descriptively.\n- Units (\u00b0C, mm, m/s for wind) are inferred, not documented in source \u2014 flagged as a caveat, worth a footnote if the report cites them externally.",
